### Step 1: Prime Number Generation

We need to generate the first \( n \) prime numbers where \( n = 20 \).

#### Algorithm to Generate First \( n \) Primes

We can use the Sieve of Eratosthenes to generate the first \( n \) primes. Here is a Java implementation:

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeGenerator {
    public static List<Integer> generateFirstNPrimes(int n) {
        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
        int limit = 100; // initial limit, will increase if necessary
        while (primes.size() < n) {
            primes.clear();
            boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[limit];
            for (int i = 2; i < limit; i++) {
                isPrime[i] = true;
            }
            for (int i = 2; i * i < limit; i++) {
                if (isPrime[i]) {
                    for (int j = i * i; j < limit; j += i) {
                        isPrime[j] = false;
                    }
                }
            }
            for (int i = 2; i < limit && primes.size() < n; i++) {
                if (isPrime[i]) {
                    primes.add(i);
                }
            }
            limit *= 2; // increase the limit and try again if not enough primes found
        }
        return primes;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 20;
        List<Integer> primes = generateFirstNPrimes(n);
        BigInteger P = BigInteger.ONE;
        int MOD = 1000000007;

        for (int prime : primes) {
            P = P.multiply(BigInteger.valueOf(prime)).mod(BigInteger.valueOf(MOD));
        }

        System.out.println("Product of first " + n + " primes mod 10^9 + 7: " + P);
    }
}
```

Running this code, we get the product of the first 20 primes modulo \( 10^9 + 7 \).

### Step 2: Modular Inverse Calculation

Using Fermat's Little Theorem, the modular inverse \( a^{-1} \mod m \) for a prime \( m \) is given by:

\[ a^{-1} \equiv a^{m-2} \mod m \]

In this case, the 15th prime number needs to be found and its modular inverse computed modulo \( 10^9 + 7 \).

#### Find the 15th Prime and Compute Its Modular Inverse

Here's the code to achieve this:

```java
public class ModularInverse {
    public static BigInteger modInverse(BigInteger a, BigInteger m) {
        return a.modPow(m.subtract(BigInteger.TWO), m);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 20;
        int k = 15;
        int MOD = 1000000007;

        List<Integer> primes = PrimeGenerator.generateFirstNPrimes(n);
        int kthPrime = primes.get(k - 1);

        BigInteger prime = BigInteger.valueOf(kthPrime);
        BigInteger mod = BigInteger.valueOf(MOD);

        BigInteger inverse = modInverse(prime, mod);
        System.out.println("Modular inverse of the " + k + "th prime " + kthPrime + " mod " + MOD + ": " + inverse);
    }
}
```

